<mxfile host="drawio" compressed="false">
  <!--
    AWS diagram skeleton: title block, internet users, AWS account, Region, VPC with two Availability
    Zones and public and private subnets, Amazon S3 in the Region outside the VPC reached through an S3
    gateway endpoint, an auxiliary-services frame, a step badge and a legend.

    Conventions this skeleton encodes:
    * Every boundary and icon style is copied verbatim from draw.io's AWS library (Sidebar-AWS4.js at
      draw.io 32.6.9: the "AWS / Groups" palette and the service palettes). Only the house typography
      (fontFamily, fontSize, fontStyle) is changed. Never retype or recolour a library style.
    * Nesting is account > Region > VPC > Availability Zone > subnet. All five are real containers
      (container=1) and every child is parented to the boundary it really sits in. Each subnet sits in
      exactly one Availability Zone.
    * The library's Availability Zone entry is a plain dashed rectangle without container=1, so the
      skeleton adds container=1;collapsible=0;recursiveResize=0 to it.
    * The auxiliary-services frame is decorative: container=0 and no children. The services drawn over
      it are parented to the Region, their real scope, and come after the frame so they draw on top.
      Library group styles carry container=1, so a frame used decoratively must set container=0.
    * Icons are 48x48 (house size) and keep the library's category fillColor and bottom label.
    * Fonts: Helvetica; title 30 bold, subtitle 16, account/Region/VPC 14 bold, Availability Zone and
      subnet 12 bold, service 10, edge 11.
    * A public subnet is public because of its route table; say so in the label, not only by colour.
    * Amazon S3 is a Regional service: a bucket lives in one Region, so it is parented to the Region,
      outside the VPC. Drawn at page level it would read as global. The S3 gateway endpoint belongs to
      the VPC (it is a route table target, not a network interface), so it sits in the VPC outside every
      Availability Zone and subnet. Traffic from the private subnets to S3 takes that route and does not
      pass through the NAT gateway. An interface endpoint is different: its network interface lives in a
      subnet, so draw it in that subnet.
    * Every edge carries a label. Twin edges (one per Availability Zone) carry the same label.

    Adding a service: copy its full style from draw.io's AWS library (the editor's shape search or the
    service palettes), keep every colour and label key, size it 48x48 and parent it to the subnet,
    Availability Zone, VPC or Region it really sits in. Do not wrap it in a category container. Confirm
    the name with check_drawio.py from the diagram-validation-and-evidence skill in strict mode. Attach
    edges to icons, never to boundaries, and use stable semantic IDs. Remove the second Availability
    Zone, the sample services, the S3 gateway endpoint or the auxiliary frame when the design does not
    have them.
  -->
  <diagram id="aws-skeleton" name="Overview">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1700" dy="1169" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1700" pageHeight="1169" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        <mxCell id="title" value="Workload name: AWS architecture" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontSize=30;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#232F3E;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="1000" height="44" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="subtitle" value="Scope, audience, source of truth and date" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontSize=16;fontColor=#545B64;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="68" width="1000" height="28" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="actor_users" value="Internet users" style="sketch=0;outlineConnect=0;fontColor=#232F3E;gradientColor=none;fillColor=#232F3D;strokeColor=none;dashed=0;verticalLabelPosition=bottom;verticalAlign=top;align=center;html=1;aspect=fixed;pointerEvents=1;shape=mxgraph.aws4.users;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontSize=10;fontStyle=0;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="390" width="48" height="48" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="account_workload" value="AWS account: workload name" style="points=[[0,0],[0.25,0],[0.5,0],[0.75,0],[1,0],[1,0.25],[1,0.5],[1,0.75],[1,1],[0.75,1],[0.5,1],[0.25,1],[0,1],[0,0.75],[0,0.5],[0,0.25]];outlineConnect=0;gradientColor=none;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;container=1;pointerEvents=0;collapsible=0;recursiveResize=0;shape=mxgraph.aws4.group;grIcon=mxgraph.aws4.group_account;strokeColor=#CD2264;fillColor=none;verticalAlign=top;align=left;spacingLeft=30;fontColor=#CD2264;dashed=0;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="180" y="130" width="1160" height="910" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
